1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Bg5 O-O (4... c5 {is a good alternative.}) 5.
e4 d6 6. Bd3 e5 7. a3 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 Nbd7 9. Ne2 Re8 10. O-O h6 11. Bxf6 Nxf6
12. Qc2 Qe7 13. Ng3 c5 {To blockade the doubled pawn.} 14. f4 Ng4 {With a dual
threat of Ne3 and Qh4.} 15. Rf3 cxd4 16. cxd4 exd4 17. Qb2 Be6 18. Qxd4 ({
Better was first} 18. h3) 18... Qf6 19. Ne2 Rac8 20. h3 Qxd4+ 21. Nxd4 Nf6 22.
Nb5 Red8 23. Nxa7 Rc5 24. Rb1 Bxc4 25. Bxc4 Rxc4 26. e5 dxe5 27. fxe5 Ne4 28.
Rxb7 Ng5 29. Re3 Rc2 30. Rb4 Rdd2 31. Rg4 Ne6 {The direct threat is 32...h5 33
R4g3 Nf4.} 32. Ree4 h5 33. Rg3 Nd4 34. Kh2 {Black menaced both ...Rc7, winning
the N, and ...Ne2+, with the gain of the exchange. After the move played, 34...
Rc7 is no use on account of 35 Rxd4.} h4 35. Rxh4 {The attacked rook cannot
move on account of ...Nf3+.} Nf5 36. Rhg4 Nxg3 37. Rxg3 Rd5 38. a4 Rxe5 39. Nb5
Ra2 40. Nc3 Ra3 41. Rd3 Kh7 42. Rf3 f6 43. Kg3 Rg5+ 44. Kf2 Rc5 {('R-RB4' -
Yorkshire Post)} 45. Ne4 Rc2+ 46. Kg3 Rxf3+ {In this elaborate endgame study
the exchange of one rook is the first step in the winning process.} 47. Kxf3
Rc4 ({There is an error in the score given in the Yorkshire Post at this point
- R-B7 (the rook is already on that square). The Mega Database 2022
interpretation as} 47... Rc7 48. a5 Ra7 {, etc, is less convincing than 47...
Rc4 48 a5 Ra4 as the newspaper shows Black's 48th move to be R-R5 (Ra4).}) 48.
a5 Ra4 49. h4 Rxa5 50. Kf4 Ra4 51. h5 Kg8 52. g4 Kf7 53. Kf5 Ra5+ 54. Kf4 Ke6
55. Nd2 Ra4+ 56. Kg3 Ke5 57. Nf3+ Ke4 58. Nd2+ Ke3 59. Nf3 Ra3 60. Ng1 Ke4+ 61.
Kh4 Ra1 62. Ne2 Kf3 63. Nd4+ Kg2 64. g5 Ra4 65. gxf6 gxf6 66. h6 Rxd4+ 67. Kh5
Kg3 68. Kg6 Rh4 {Source: Yorkshire Post, 10 June 1929, with notes by Yates.}
0-1

