1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e5 Nfd7 6. Bxe7 Qxe7 7. Qd2 a6 8.
Nd1 c5 9. c3 {Mr. Yates deals with the French defence on the lines recommended
by the late Richard Teichmann whose judgment was highly respected by other
masters.} Nc6 10. f4 cxd4 ({Premature; preferable is} 10... O-O {followed by ..
.f6.}) 11. cxd4 Qb4 {Nor is this good strategy, Black being left with an
endgame which he can scarcely hope to win; and, as Dr. Lasker once remarked to
the writer "If you want to draw you must play to win."} 12. Nf3 Qxd2+ 13. Kxd2
Nb6 14. Ne3 Bd7 15. Rc1 Rc8 16. Bd3 O-O ({Perhaps} 16... Ke7 {might have been
better.}) 17. h4 Nb4 18. Bb1 Nc4+ ({This move lets in the adverse knight at d6.
The most promising chance, for a draw, was} 18... Rxc1 19. Rxc1 (19. Kxc1 Rc8+)
) 19. Nxc4 dxc4 20. Ng5 h6 21. Ne4 b5 22. Nd6 {The great Anderssen used to say,
Once firmly establish a knight on your sixth and you may safely go to sleep.
The rest will play itself!"} Rc7 23. g4 Nd5 24. f5 Nb6 25. Rhg1 Bc6 26. g5 h5
27. g6 f6 28. Rcf1 fxe5 29. dxe5 exf5 30. Bxf5 Re7 31. Nf7 Bd5 32. e6 Bxe6 33.
Bxe6 Rxe6 34. Rg5 Rf6 35. Rxh5 {Decisive. Mr. Yates' conduct of the endgame
was both instructive and entertaining.} Rd8+ 36. Kc2 {Sources: Sunday Times,
23 June 1929; BCM, July 1929, p272; 101 of My Best Games by Yates} 1-0

